  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to employ a particle swarm optimization (PSO) algorithm to explore the solution space more effectively than gradient-based methods. PSO is well-suited for non-convex optimization and avoids local optima by maintaining a population of candidate solutions. This approach addresses the limitation of the previous method, which could get trapped in local minima by using a global search strategy. The key advantage is the ability to balance exploration and exploitation while maintaining constraint feasibility. The primary limitation is computational cost, which is mitigated by using a smaller population size and efficient constraint handling.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nVariables:\n- h \u2208 [0,1]^n_points: step function with values in [0,1]\n- dx = 2.0 / n_points: grid spacing\n- target_sum = n_points / 2: required integral constraint\n- C\u2085 = max_k [\u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1 - h(x + k)) dx] * dx\n\nObjective: Minimize C\u2085\nConstraints:\n1. \u2211h[i] = target_sum\n2. 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n\nPSO Algorithm:\n- Initialize a population of h_vectors with n_particles individuals\n- Each particle has position (h) and velocity (v)\n- Evaluate fitness as C\u2085 for each particle\n- Update personal and global best positions\n- Ensure constraint feasibility by normalizing h vectors after updates\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nInitialization:\n- If initial_h_values is provided, create a population with one particle from it\n- Otherwise, generate a random population with h[i] ~ uniform(0,1), then normalize to target_sum\n\nPSO Loop:\nFor each iteration until time expires:\na) Evaluate fitness (C\u2085) for all particles\nb) Update personal best positions\nc) Update global best position\nd) For each particle, update velocity and position using:\n   v = \u03c9*v + \u03c6_p * r1*(pbest - current) + \u03c6_g * r2*(gbest - current)\n   h = current + v\ne) Normalize h to satisfy \u2211h = target_sum:\n   h = h * (target_sum / np.sum(h))\nf) Clip values to [0,1] and re-normalize if necessary\ng) Track best solution found so far\n\nTermination:\n- After time budget is exhausted or convergence\n- Return the best solution, its C\u2085 value, and n_points\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault Configuration:\n- n_particles = 30 (balance exploration and computation)\n- n_points = 400 (sufficient resolution without overwhelming time)\n- Inertia weight \u03c9 = 0.7\n- Cognitive coefficient \u03c6_p = 1.5\n- Social coefficient \u03c6_g = 1.5\n- Randomness factors r1, r2 ~ Uniform(0,1)\n- Maximum iterations = 1000 (adjust based on time)\n- Mutation probability = 5% (random perturbation to escape local optima)\n\nVariations:\n- Hybrid approach: After PSO, refine the best solution with local optimization\n- Adaptive parameters: Adjust \u03c9 and \u03c6 during runtime based on convergence\n- Multi-population strategy: Use multiple PSO swarms with different initializations\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nTime Allocation:\n- 400s: PSO optimization\n- 200s: Refinement and fallback\n\nRobustness Measures:\n- Maintain valid solutions by normalizing after every update\n- Track the best solution throughout\n- Use exponential decay for velocity to stabilize convergence\n- Limit particle updates to prevent unnecessary computation\n- Store the parent solution as a fallback if PSO fails to improve\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFeasibility Checks:\n- \u2211h \u2248 target_sum within 1e-6\n- All h[i] \u2208 [0,1]\n- Correlation calculation uses mode=\"full\" with proper scaling\n\nFinal Validation:\n- Re-compute C\u2085 using exact objective function\n- Ensure results match evaluator's calculation\n- Perform sanity checks:\n   a) Correlation length matches 2n_points-1\n   b) Overlap values are positive\n   c) Maximum overlap is correctly identified\n\nReturn Format:\n- h_values as list of floats in [0,1]\n- c5_bound as exact value\n- n_points as integer",
